  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The best choice is <b>Implement a standby deployment in a secondary AWS region and use Route 53 failover routing to direct users to the standby in the event the main site becomes unavailable</b>. This is known as a warm standby or active/passive DR pattern, which allows for quick failover with pre-provisioned resources, meeting a 1-hour RTO requirement for the entire multi-tier application. A warm standby allows for faster recovery compared to re-deployment or recreating from snapshots, since resources are already running and only need to be promoted or pointed to through DNS failover.<br><br>Looking at the other options:<br><br><i>Set up frequent EBS snapshots using EBS Lifecycle Manager to capture your volumes each hour</i> focuses on protecting EBS volumes but does not address recovery of the whole stack, nor does it ensure the rapid recovery of application and database layers as required in a cross-region scenario.<br><br><i>Configure your RDS instances to take automated hourly snapshots and replicate them to another region</i> increases database durability, but this technique alone does not guarantee the availability of compute and frontend resources in another region, and restoration from snapshots can take longer than desired for a strict RTO.<br><br><i>Create a Jenkins automation that generates AMIs for your EC2 servers every hour and triggers them through a scheduled pipeline</i> provides automated server image creation, but it does not solve rapid, full application recovery by itself—re-deploying everything from AMIs can be slower than failing over to a warm standby.<br><br><b>Tip:</b> For strict RTOs, a DR setup that maintains running resources in a failover region (warm standby or even active-active) is the most effective way to ensure your application can be restored within the required window.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You are a reliability engineer at a company that has recently deployed an application on AWS. The application architecture consists of three layers: frontend, backend, and database. You're utilizing services such as EC2, ELB, Auto Scaling, Route53, and RDS. The business requires that the application be recoverable within 1 hour should a disruption occur (a 1-hour RTO). <br><br> Which strategy best ensures you can meet this recovery time objective for the entire application?</p>",
